        <template><![CDATA[<div class="x-casemgmt-case-confirmation-widget">
    <!--
        The heading is <h2> rather than <h3>. This panel replaces the
        submission form in place, and the page's single <h1> ("Submit a
        Case") is rendered by the parent submission widget OUTSIDE its
        ng-if, so it survives the swap. <h2> therefore continues the
        page's heading outline without skipping a level, which an <h3>
        under an <h1> would do. The TEXT is unchanged and must stay
        unchanged - "Your case has been submitted" is verbatim per AAP
        Section 0.4.4.

        role="alert" on the panel is what announces the confirmation to
        assistive technology: the panel is inserted into the DOM by the
        parent's ng-if only after a successful POST, and an alert region
        is announced on insertion.
    -->
    <div class="alert alert-success" role="alert">
        <h2>Your case has been submitted</h2>
        <p ng-if="c.options.number">
            Case number: <strong>{{c.options.number}}</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
            Please save this case number for future reference. You can use it to look up the status of your case at any time.
        </p>
        <!--
            The link that makes the previous sentence actionable.

            The panel hands the requester a case number and tells them
            they can look up its status, and until this anchor existed
            there was no way to reach the page that consumes it: the
            requester had to know, and hand-type,
            /x_casemgmt_case_portal?id=x_casemgmt_case_status. AAP Section
            0.4.4 describes the submission page and the status page as one
            portal experience, so the confirmation is exactly where the
            crossing belongs.

            The href is RELATIVE ('?id=...'), which keeps the requester on
            whichever portal instance served this page and needs no
            knowledge of the portal's URL suffix - so it is correct on
            /x_casemgmt_case_portal and on any other portal that embeds
            this widget, and it carries no hardcoded instance host.
            x_casemgmt_case_status is the <id> of
            ../pages/sp_page_x_casemgmt_case_status.xml, resolved by that
            slug and not by sys_id.

            btn / btn-default / btn-primary and alert-link are all bundled
            Bootstrap 3 classes shipped with the Service Portal, so this
            adds no branding and needs no styling rule, per AAP Section
            0.4.4's default-theme mandate. This widget's <css> element
            carries exactly one rule, and it is not about this link's
            appearance: it restores the keyboard focus ring that the
            theme paints transparent. See the DEFAULT THEME note in the
            record description above.

            The case number is appended as a `number` query parameter when
            one is known. The status page CONSUMES it: the lookup widget
            pre-fills its input from that parameter and runs the lookup
            once on load, so following this link lands the requester on
            their own case's status rather than on an empty form they
            have to re-type into. The parameter is optional on both ends
            - the second anchor below omits it, and the lookup widget
            degrades to an empty form when it is absent - so neither side
            depends on the other.
        -->
        <p>
            <a ng-if="c.options.number"
               ng-href="?id=x_casemgmt_case_status&amp;number={{c.options.number}}">Check the status of this case</a>
            <a ng-if="!c.options.number"
               href="?id=x_casemgmt_case_status">Check the status of a case</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
]]></template>
    </sp_widget>
